Third-party components
----------------------

The extension is shipped as bundles: `out/extension.js` and `out/mcp-server.js`
(extension host and MCP server, esbuild) and `out/webview/**` (webview UI, Vite).
Third-party code is compiled into those bundles, so the packaged `.vsix` does not
contain `node_modules` and ships no dependency license file of its own. The only
license files in the `.vsix` are this `NOTICE` and the repository's `LICENSE`; the
full license text of each bundled package stays in that package's own distribution
(the npm registry tarball, or the copy under `node_modules` in a checkout).

Packages whose code is compiled into the bundles:

  vue                           MIT
  vue-router                    MIT
  vue-i18n                      MIT
  @vscode-elements/elements     MIT
  easymde                       MIT
  tributejs                     MIT
  dompurify                     MPL-2.0 OR Apache-2.0 (used under Apache-2.0)
  zod                           MIT
  @modelcontextprotocol/sdk     MIT
  undici                        MIT

These are the packages the sources import directly; their own dependencies (for
example `codemirror`, `marked`, `typo-js` and `codemirror-spell-checker`, which
`easymde` bundles into its distributed `easymde.min.js`) are compiled in with them
and are covered by their upstream licenses. `pnpm licenses list --prod` is the
authoritative, current list of every license in the production dependency graph.

Build- and test-only dependencies (Vite, Vitest, esbuild, TypeScript, `@vscode/vsce`,
jsdom, msw, and `lightningcss`, which Vite uses as its CSS transformer because
`webview/vite.config.ts` sets `css.transformer: 'lightningcss'`) are not bundled into
the shipped files and are not part of the extension's runtime. They do appear in
`pnpm licenses list --prod` because pnpm walks the whole workspace graph — see
"Lightning CSS" below.

